---
/**
 * 25 · Rosette, Five
 *
 * Five circles, which is the one count with no mirror pair top to bottom,
 * so the pentafoil sits point-up and the two halves of the mark are
 * genuinely different shapes.
 *
 * The odd count also rules out a circular centre: an odd-row silhouette
 * cannot hold an even-row circle with equal air above and below. So this
 * is the only one of the four whose bright field is the *deep overlap
 * itself* — the region all five petals cover. The five-pointed shape the
 * face sits on is the rule made visible, not a disc laid on top of it.
 *
 * Five is also the most constrained count on this grid. The face has to
 * split both the field and the whole mark into equal air, and with five
 * petals almost nothing does: a full sweep of petal size, ring distance,
 * phase, centre and depth threshold leaves one ring distance standing.
 * So the petals here sit closer in than the six or the eight, and the
 * mark earns its shape from the five-pointed field rather than from a
 * deeply lobed rim. That is a real limit of the geometry, not a choice.
 *
 * What it does have is light. Four even steps run from a mid teal at the
 * rim to the palest tone in the set at the middle, and because the field
 * is the five-fold overlap rather than a disc, that palest tone arrives
 * in the shape of a five-pointed star.
 *
 * Soft mint — the least saturated of the set.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

<MarkFrame size={size} title="Hozz — Rosette, Five">
